The front camera: an S5K3T2 driver with the phone's own tables

WHY THE CAMERA IS BEING SWITCHED
--------------------------------
Patch 0050 left the rear IMX682 identified, transmitting and with the CAMSS
pipeline validating in full. And even so not a single frame arrived, not even
with the sensor's internal bar pattern.

Now it is known why, and it was no driver fault: the rear camera is wired in
C-PHY, and CAMSS only speaks D-PHY. It is measured in the surya's own factory
calibration file, by two independent paths that agree:

    laneAssign = 0x0210    three lanes -> C-PHY trios
    0x0114     = 0x02      three lanes, in ALL FIVE mode tables

The front camera, on the other hand, says laneAssign = 0x3210 and
0x0114 = 0x0300: four D-PHY lanes. And so do the others. The detail is in
surya/docs/CAMERA-04-rear-is-cphy.md.

(Along the way it corrects a note from the previous handover which read that
register as 0x0111 and concluded D-PHY. The register is 0x0114 and it says the
opposite.)

WHAT THIS PATCH BRINGS
----------------------
1. drivers/media/i2c/s5k3t2.c, a complete v4l2_subdev for the 20 MP Samsung
   S5K3T2, with two modes: 2592x1940 binned and 5184x3880 full.
2. The device tree: the front camera on cci0_i2c1 against csiphy1, and the rear
   one out.
3. camss.c: it raises the bandwidth request to the bus, without which the
   stream stops after a few seconds (see below).

THE TABLES ARE FROM THIS PHONE, NOT FROM ANOTHER
------------------------------------------------
They come from com.qti.sensormodule.j20c_sunny_s5k3t2_front.bin, that is from
the same module this device carries, read with
surya/camara/leer-sensormodule.py. Nothing like the rear camera, where they had
to be brought from MediaTek's GPL driver for another phone and the PLL
recalculated. Here nothing is recalculated.

To read them the format of the regSetting block had to be deciphered, as it was
not documented: it is an array of u64, ten per register, with the address at
index 2 of each group; the values go separately, in the registerData
descriptors that follow, one per register and in the same order.

THE LINK ARITHMETIC, AND HOW IT WAS CHECKED
-------------------------------------------
With INCK = 19.2 MHz and the PLL registers from the tables:

    vt_pix_clk = 19.2 / 3 (0x0304) * 185 (0x0306) / 1 (0x0302) / 7 (0x0300)
               = 169.14 MHz
    pixel_rate = vt_pix_clk * 4 pixels per cycle = 676.57 MHz
    link_freq  = pixel_rate * 10 bits / (2 * 4 lanes) = 845.71 MHz

The "4 pixels per cycle" factor is not assumed: it was calibrated with the
sibling driver s5k3m5.c from this same tree, which declares 602.5 MHz and whose
tables give exactly that figure with this formula.

And it adds up by a second path, the physical one: in the full mode
5184 x 10 / 4 = 12960 bits per lane and line are needed, which at 1691 Mbit/s
take 7.66 us, within the 8.04 us the line lasts. A 5 % margin.

Both modes come out at 30.1 fps, which is what is declared by someone who has
already made this sensor capture on this same SoC.

And 845.71 MHz fits comfortably in the SM7150's CSIPHY, which is where the
first attempt with the rear camera died: its timer clock stops at 300 MHz and
the driver asks for link_freq/4 below that. 845.71/4 = 211 MHz.

THE BUS BANDWIDTH
-----------------
icc_res_sm7150 asked for 2 GiB/s, and with that the CSI-2 receiver runs out of
FIFO after a few seconds as soon as the CPU touches memory -which is exactly
what libcamera does when developing in software-. It is raised to 4 GiB/s,
which is the figure measured to hold up cleanly. It comes from PR #59 of
sm7150-mainline/linux, done on a Xiaomi Mi 9T with this same SoC and this same
sensor.

THE TWO UNKNOWNS, AND WHY THEY GO AS PARAMETERS
-----------------------------------------------
This phone hangs on roughly one boot in four, so spending a reboot per
hypothesis is expensive. The two things that cannot be settled by reading go as
module parameters, and are tested with rmmod/insmod:

  i2c_addr     The factory file does NOT carry the sensor's address: its only
               slaveAddr is 0xa0, that is the EEPROM at 0x50 -the same trap
               that already cost time with the rear camera, where the
               "documented" 0x50 was the EEPROM and the sensor was at 0x1a-. By
               default the driver looks for it by reading the identification
               (0x3142) and relocates itself.
  mipi_switch  The front camera and the wide angle share CSIPHY1 through a MIPI
               switch and it is not known which position is which. By default it
               is not touched.
  bayer        The mosaic order cannot be deduced from the factory file. Getting
               it wrong only spoils the colour, it does not prevent capturing.

